WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.762
Why chain a temple pillar that cannot even move?

2
00:00:04.042 --> 00:00:14.258
At Wat Wiang, in Thoen, Lampang, a legend surrounds one pillar. After Luang Pu Saen Kham restored the temple, strange stories began.

3
00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:27.417
By morning, duckweed, algae, and snails clung to its base. Picture it: things from a pond, stuck to wood holding up a temple. How did they get there?

4
00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:32.327
The pond in this story lay about two kilometers away.

5
00:00:32.625 --> 00:00:40.473
Legend calls it Nong Thuam. A tree spirit was said to live inside the pillar, and visit that pond to bathe.

6
00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:53.445
Night belonged to the spirit and the water. By dawn, the story returned to the pillar, with duckweed, algae, and snails: tiny traces linking the pond to the temple.

7
00:00:54.625 --> 00:01:00.012
So Luang Pu Saen Kham bound the pillar with a spell, and chained its base.

8
00:01:00.458 --> 00:01:03.570
Chained already... so why remove it anyway?

9
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:11.755
After the spell and the chain, the legend introduces another inhabitant: bees, nesting inside the hollow wood.

10
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:22.235
Now look out from that hollow. Back then, forest bordered the temple. Honey inside the pillar tempted a bear to claw its way in. Here began the reason for replacing it.

11
00:01:24.208 --> 00:01:28.295
The bear reached the hollow. What happened next?

12
00:01:28.792 --> 00:01:34.587
It clawed for honey until the wood split. So Luang Pu Saen Kham had the pillar removed.

13
00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:49.532
In its place came a brick-and-plaster pillar. Picture that change: hollow wood with a beehive, replaced by masonry, as the story tells it. That answers why even a chained pillar had to be replaced.

14
00:01:52.208 --> 00:01:55.180
Know another temple with a chained tree or pillar?

15
00:01:55.792 --> 00:02:02.015
Comment the temple and district, or tell us why it was chained. LokSon may explore that story next.

16
00:02:02.684 --> 00:02:05.192
Wat Wiang’s story begins with one question...
